From 4df351107c35722cb47a3a11229e6076bf73c426 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Milad <mmaziesf@uwaterloo.ca>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 23:03:15 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Muse voice: spoken replies, local speech-to-text,
 hands-free turns, wake word

Turns the gadget into a hands-free voice assistant backed by a speech
server of your own (glasses-bridge's speech_server.py or any server with
the same /stt, /tts and /health endpoints). Everything is off by default;
the Core2 v1.1 profile turns it on.

- MUSE_TTS: each reply is spoken by your text-to-speech server: MP3
  streamed sentence by sentence as the reply arrives, captions following
  the speech. A short filler ("Hmm, let me think.") is spoken while Muse
  thinks. Muted speaker or unreachable server: the reply shows as text.
- MUSE_LOCAL_STT: the recording is transcribed on your server and sent to
  Muse as text with MUSE_VOICE_NOTE_HINT; if the server fails it goes up
  as a voice note as before.
- Faster end of speech: at the first 200 ms pause the speech so far is
  transcribed early, and the turn's end reuses that transcript when
  nothing more was said (Muse gets the text ~0.1-0.2 s after you stop
  instead of ~0.6 s). A transcript that reads finished ends the turn at
  once; one that reads unfinished, or is only the wake word, waits up
  to 1.5 s for the rest.
- MUSE_HANDS_FREE: a tap starts a turn that ends when you stop talking
  (holding still works as push-to-talk), detected against the room's
  floor and the speaker's level; optional follow-up window
  (MUSE_FOLLOW_UP_S, 0 on the Core2: every turn starts with the wake word).
- MUSE_WAKE_WORD: on-device "Hey Jarvis" (microWakeWord v2 model,
  Apache-2.0) on TFLite Micro, with the TF audio microfrontend vendored
  in components/microfrontend (esp-tflite-micro 1.4.1 no longer ships
  it). The 18 KB arena is in PSRAM: the Core2 reallocates its I2S DMA
  buffers on every mic/speaker switch and an internal arena starved them.
  The streaming model and frontend are reset at every turn so the wake
  word just heard can't fire again.
- Speech server connections: one per endpoint, opened with GET /health as
  a turn starts listening (so a TLS handshake overlaps the question),
  reused for the turn's requests, and freed when the turn ends: their
  buffers fall under the 4 KB always-internal malloc threshold, and kept
  between turns they took the internal RAM the display's DMA transfers
  need (the avatar froze). A connection that fails before any response
  is retried on a new client. https:// URLs are verified against the CA
  bundle, TLS sessions are resumed (session tickets), and
  MUSE_SPEECH_TOKEN is sent as a bearer token.
- muse_voice_set_turn_hook lets the app act as each turn starts.
